            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<h4>1. Prioritize the Air Around You <\/h4><p><\/p><p>Air is the most essential — yet often overlooked — part of our home environment. Beyond filtering dust and allergens, air wellness means conditioning and optimizing the air, so it actively supports sleep, healthy skin, energy, and overall vitality. <\/p><ul><li>Focus on solutions that go beyond purification to create balanced, health-optimized air.  <\/li><li>Think of air care the same way you think about hydration or nutrition — as a daily foundation of wellness. <\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><h4><strong>2. Choose Health-Conscious Materials<\/strong> <\/h4><p> <\/p><p>The surfaces, fabrics, and finishes in your home directly can emit compounds that affect indoor air quality and your wellbeing. To reduce harmful exposures: <\/p><ul><li>Select low-VOC (volatile, organic compounds) paints and finishes to avoid unnecessary chemicals. <\/li><li>Opt for solid wood furniture over particleboard, which can release formaldehyde, a chemical that can irritate the lungs. <\/li><li>Use organic or untreated textiles for bedding and curtains. <\/li><\/ul><p> <\/p><h4><strong>3. Design Restorative Spaces for Sleep<\/strong> <\/h4><p> <\/p><p>Sleep is where your body repairs and recharges — but indoor toxins and poor air quality can quietly disrupt it. A restorative sleep sanctuary should include: <\/p><ul><li>Clean, optimized air that helps you fall asleep faster and wake refreshed. <\/li><li>Thoughtful design choices like clutter-free layouts, natural fabrics, and blackout curtains. <\/li><li>A cool, well-ventilated room that supports deep rest. <\/li><\/ul><p><\/p>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
